  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   Central idea: We propose to combine level-based optimization with iterated local search to escape local minima and gradually blacken the upper bound.\n   Rationale: The provided initial solution may be stuck in a suboptimal basin. By increasing the grid resolution (number of grid points) and solving a more difficult but representative optimization problem at each level, we can systematically lower C. The iterated local search family provides better exploratory capability than basic hill climbing, maintaining local intensity while using simulated annealing as an occasional escape mechanism. The key innovation is the adaptive resolution control governed by a time weighting factor.\n\n   Important limitations:\n   - We assume that the continuous optimum can be approached by sufficiently coarse discretization.\n   - The function evaluations are computationally expensive (O(n_points^2)) so we limit the search space through careful time allocation between levels.\n   - The solution may be constrained by floating point precision in the correlation calculation.\n   - We use a bounded optimization method that may not find proven global optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Representation: h is represented as an array of length n_points in the interval [0,2] with dx = 2.0/n_points.\n   Objective: minimize C5 = max_{k} [ dx * np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")[k] ]\n   Constraints: \n     f_i(h) = 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n     g(h) = np.sum(h) * dx - 1 = 0\n   Continuous version: The original continuous problem is defined on [0,2] for h(x) with \u222bh(x)dx=1. Our discrete approximation uses:\n     \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx \u2248 dx * np.correlate(h,1-h, mode=\"full\")[k]\n\n   Since our function is non-convex, we use an equality-constrained optimization via:\n     minimize obj(h) = max_{k} [dx * corr_k]\n     s.t. 0<=h[i]<=1, \u03a3h_i * dx = 1\n\n   Relaxation: We allow violation of constraints with an L2 penalty to make the problem tractable for gradient-based methods, but repair solutions that exceed constraints before evaluation.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Initialize:\n     Base-level: start at level0 with n0 = 70, and work upwards until time expires.\n     At each level n:\n        - Start with upward transfer from parent at level0 (if available)\n        - Initialize: Use the provided initial_h_values if transfer succeeds, otherwise use uniform initialization\n        - Apply constraints with repair: clip to [0,1] and scale to meet \u03a3h_i * dx = 1\n        - Solve with SCIMP (Sigopt) using:\n            * iterated local search: 1000 steps with adaptive temperature (initial 1.0, decayed by 0.95)\n            * repair integrated: max 5 violation tolerance steps\n        - Track best solution found at this level\n        - Time budget: Level0: 500 seconds / level (for higher levels, reduce time per level)\n        - Progress tracking: global_best, current_level, level_timeouts\n\n   Transfer mechanism:\n     From a lower resolution n1 to a higher n2:\n        - Align parent's function values with child grid via bilinear interpolation\n        - Ensure continuity at boundaries by preserving values in overlap regions\n\n   Repair schema:\n     For a candidate h at grid size n:\n        clipped = np.clip(h, epsilon, 1-epsilon)   # use small epsilon to avoid 0/1 boundary\n        integral = np.sum(clipped) * dx\n        if abs(integral - 1) > 0.01 * dx: \n            # Scale while maintaining interval\n            scalar = (1/(dx))/integral\n            candidate = clipped * scalar\n\n   Objective scaling:\n      C5 = dx * np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))\n      But in the code we compute:\n        C5 = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")) * dx   # matches parent evaluator\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Optimization path: exponential grid increase (n*1.5, n*2, etc.) to avoid too rapid dimension increase\n   - Level-bound time: level_i allotment_time = base_time * (1/2)^(relative_level+1)\n   - Hill climbing parameters:\n        max_iteration = 1000\n        temp_initial = 1.5\n        temp_decay = 0.95\n        temp_min = 0.1\n        neighbor_std = 0.01 (for sampling jitter)\n   - Constraints: \n        bound_violation_cost = 100.0 * n_points\n        constraint_penalty = 100.0 * np.sum(np.maximum(0, h - 1) + np.maximum(0, -h)) + \n                  100.0 * abs(1 - (np.sum(h) * dx))\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Time estimation:\n        n_points^2 operations per correlation (significant cost)\n        Exponential grid increase to prevent catastrophic dimensionality\n        Expect to do 3-4 level increases (50, 75, 112, 168 points) within 1300s (with level0 at n0=70)\n   - Important details:\n        Warm start each level with parent found in previous level\n        If central optimal point not found, fall back to provided parent (n0=70)\n        Two-phase repair: first clip (to [0,1]), then scale to match integral\n        Out-of-bound handling: repair will penalize solutions stronger than basic clipping\n   - Response to failure:\n        If time runs out at level i, return best found so far.\n        If gradient-based optimizer fails during interpolation, use fallback if interpolation exceeds class bounds.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Validate all returned h_values for:\n        (i) 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n        (ii) np.abs((np.sum(h)*dx) - 1) < tolerance\n   - Evaluate C5 exactly using parent's correlate method (dx * max correlation)\n   - Fallback: if interpolation produces out-of-bound values, use simple grid refining from parent at current level\n   - Contract: optimize over continuous function with finite grid representation.",
